[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m c_src/src/driver.c:7:// modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense,      <- licence comment
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m c_src/include/driver.h:7:// modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense,  <- licence comment
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m c_src/include/driver.h:24:#ifndef DRIVER_H_                                  <- include guard
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m c_src/include/driver.h:31:#endif //DRIVER_H_                                 <- include guard
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Mechanical finding: **the C library contains no error-return statement, no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `return` at all, no `assert`, no range check, no null check, no error enum,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and no min/max constant.** Both public functions return `void`. There is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m therefore no error *code* surface to compare.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m What remains are the implicit rejection/normalisation behaviours the C code
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m actually performs on invalid or out-of-range input. Each is one row.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | test |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|----------|---------------------------------------------|-------------------|------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E1 | `driver` | `x` has bits above bit 1 set (any value `> 3`, up to `UINT_MAX`) | no error; silently truncated by `and $0x3` — prints `x & 3` | `err_e1_x_out_of_range` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E2 | `driver` | `y` has bits above bit 2 set (any value `> 7`, up to `UINT_MAX`) | no error; silently truncated by `and $0x7` — prints `y & 7` | `err_e2_y_out_of_range` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E3 | `driver` | `b` byte is neither 0 nor 1 (an out-of-range `_Bool`, e.g. `2`, `0xFE`, `0xFF`) — the FFI analogue of an out-of-range enum value | no error; `movzbl` + `and $0x1` — prints `b & 1`. `0xFE` prints `0`, `0xFF` prints `1` | `err_e3_bool_out_of_range` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E4 | `driver` | `z == INT_MIN` / `z == INT_MAX` / `z < 0` (one step past, and at, the `int` range ends) | no error; `z` is a full-width `int` field, printed verbatim with `%d` | `err_e4_z_extremes` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E5 | `print_foo` | `foo` byte 0 has bits 6–7 set (padding bits inside the bit-field allocation unit, unreachable through `driver`) | no error; those bits belong to no member and are not read — output depends only on bits 0–5 | `err_e5_padding_bits_ignored` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E6 | `print_foo` | `foo` bytes 1–3 hold arbitrary garbage (struct padding, left uninitialised by `driver`) | no error; padding is never read — output unaffected | `err_e6_struct_padding_ignored` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E7 | `print_foo` | `foo == NULL` | unconditional dereference → `SIGSEGV`. Not a recoverable error; the C returns no code | `err_e7_null_pointer_segv` (both `.so`s crash with the same signal, checked in forked child processes) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
